<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PathAssistant xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        The SELL-SIDE NDA path, for the Disposition_NDA record type.
        Four steps: Not Sent -> Sent -> Signed, plus Declined as a terminal off-ramp
        (Part 2 lines 28-41).

        NONE OF THIS TEXT IS CARRIED FORWARD, and that is correct rather than a lapse of the
        copy-byte-identical discipline: only the Sent and Signed VALUES are shared with the
        acquisition path, and both mean something different here. On an acquisition DPEG is the
        party receiving confidential information and signing; on a disposition DPEG is the party
        DISCLOSING it, collecting one NDA from the prospective buyer and one from the
        introducing broker, and counter-signing both. The acquisition Signed text names
        "advancing the deal to Underwriting", an Opportunity stage no disposition NDA has.

        DECLINED IS A TERMINAL OFF-RAMP, NOT A FORWARD STEP. It renders last, the same shape as
        Dead/Pass on the Opportunity path, and is accepted as such. It is deliberately the only
        step with no <fieldNames>: the NDA fields it would surface (Date_Signed__c,
        Counter_Signed_Date__c) are precisely the ones that must stay blank, and Notes__c is a
        Long Text Area, which is not a supported Path key field.

        MOVING A SIGNED NDA TO DECLINED IS SAFE AND IS THE INTENDED RECOVERY: the before-save
        flow NDA_Signed_Status_Sync defaults everything that is not Signed to
        NDA_Signed__c = FALSE, and it never clears Date_Signed__c - so the historical signature
        date is retained while the record stops counting as signed.

        THE RELEASE GATE LIVES ON THE PARENT, NOT HERE. Nothing is shared with a prospective
        buyer until EVERY NDA on the disposition that was not DECLINED reads Signed; that is
        enforced by All_NDAs_Signed_Before_Progression on Disposition__c, which blocks every
        forward stage from NDA onward on an Off_Market disposition. The step text below says so,
        because the Path is where a user will look first when the stage refuses to move.

        ⚠ THE DECLINED EXCLUSION IS NEW (2026-08-09, DECISION D20/C2) AND IT CHANGED WHAT THE
        DECLINED STEP MEANS. That step used to end "delete this NDA rather than leaving it
        Declined", because a declined NDA counted toward the outstanding total and blocked the
        disposition forever. D20 refuses deletion - allowDelete stays FALSE on NDA__c, since a
        declined NDA is the audit evidence of a party who refused to sign - and fixes the
        arithmetic instead, in NDA_Signed_Rollup's Is_Row_Signed decision, which now counts a
        Declined row toward NEITHER counter. The step text was corrected in the same change.

        THAT ALSO PROMOTES THE Signed -> Declined RECOVERY DESCRIBED BELOW from a curiosity to
        THE ONLY WAY to take a party back out of the release gate's arithmetic. Marking an NDA
        Declined - not deleting it - is now the sanctioned exit, which is why
        NDA__c.Is_Decline_Allowed__c is true at Signed as well as at Not Sent and Sent.

        Pending and Received do not appear because the Disposition_NDA record type restricts
        them out; they belong to NDA_Path_Acquisition.

        DocuSign is not licensed yet (decision D2) - these states are driven by hand and the
        executed file is attached to the record. Method__c already offers DocuSign, so an
        envelope callback can drive the same states later without reshaping this path.

        STEP ORDER IS ENFORCED BY THE MASTER VALUE SET, NOT BY THIS FILE, FOR THE PATH BUBBLES.
        Runtime PATH display order comes from the Status__c picklist value order (sorted=false);
        the order in this file is only for readability.

        ⚠ AMENDED 2026-08-16 - THE CLAIM THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO MAKE IS NOW FALSE AND IS RETRACTED
        RATHER THAN SILENTLY LEFT TO MISLEAD. It asserted the master set was "deliberately ordered
        Pending, Not Sent, Sent, Received, Signed, Declined so that BOTH record types render
        correctly with one ordering: acquisition sees Pending -> Sent -> Received -> Signed and
        disposition sees Not Sent -> Sent -> Signed -> Declined." The acquisition NDA sequence was
        REVERSED the same day (commit 804e688): acquisition now runs
        Pending -> Received -> Signed -> Sent, with 'Sent' TERMINAL rather than a middle step, and
        this file's own sequence (Not Sent -> Sent -> Signed -> Declined, below) is unchanged. The
        master field's value order was DELIBERATELY NOT touched by that reversal (see the decision
        recorded in NDA_Path_Acquisition's own comment) - which means the "one master order serves
        both" claim this paragraph made is no longer even attempted, let alone true: the master
        order (Pending, Not Sent, Sent, Received, Signed, Declined) puts acquisition's 'Signed'
        ahead of 'Sent', the OPPOSITE of the reversed acquisition sequence's ... Signed -> Sent.

        🔴 THE UNDERLYING CONSTRAINT IS REAL AND IS THE REASON NO FIX WAS ATTEMPTED HERE:
        acquisition and disposition need OPPOSITE orders on the values they SHARE. Acquisition now
        ends ... Signed -> Sent; disposition ends ... Sent -> Signed. ONE master picklist order
        cannot satisfy both directions for the same shared pair at once, so there is no reordering
        of Status__c that fixes the acquisition side without breaking this one.

        WHAT IS STILL GENUINELY UNVERIFIED, RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN ASSUMED IN EITHER DIRECTION:
        whether the Path's bubble order and the Status__c field's edit-form DROPDOWN order are
        actually driven by this master field's value order at all, versus by each record type's
        own picklistValues order (Acquisition_NDA's was reordered to Pending, Received, Signed,
        Sent in the same follow-up that added this comment) or by each PathAssistant file's own
        <pathAssistantSteps> order. No in-org check was performed to distinguish these. See
        NDA_Path_Acquisition's own comment for the fuller statement of this open question and its
        stated fallback - the Path bubbles in BOTH files are independently confirmed to already
        list the correct sequence for their own record type regardless of which mechanism turns
        out to govern rendering, so nothing here depends on resolving it.

        NOT SENT SITS SECOND IN THE MASTER SET ON PURPOSE, AND MOVING IT IS SAFE. It was
        originally appended fifth, which rendered this path as Sent, Signed, Not Sent, Declined -
        putting the STARTING state third. That is not cosmetic: a Path is a progress indicator,
        so it told the user the process begins at Sent, and it disagreed with the step order in
        this very file. Placing it ahead of Sent cannot disturb the acquisition path because
        Acquisition_NDA RESTRICTS NOT SENT OUT ENTIRELY - a value a record type does not expose
        cannot occupy a position in that record type's rendering. Anyone re-ordering this
        picklist again must re-check that claim against BOTH record type files.
    -->
    <active>true</active>
    <entityName>NDA__c</entityName>
    <fieldName>Status__c</fieldName>
    <masterLabel>NDA Path Disposition</masterLabel>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <fieldNames>Counterparty_Name__c</fieldNames>
        <fieldNames>Party_Role__c</fieldNames>
        <fieldNames>Method__c</fieldNames>
        <info>An interested party has been identified but the NDA has not gone out yet. Record who they are and whether this is the prospective Buyer or the Introducing Broker who brought them - DPEG collects one NDA per party.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Not Sent</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <fieldNames>Date_Sent__c</fieldNames>
        <fieldNames>OneDrive_URL__c</fieldNames>
        <info>NDA sent to this party. Track the days out and chase it - nothing about the property is shared with anyone until every NDA on this disposition is Signed, so an outstanding NDA holds the whole disposition at the NDA stage.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Sent</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <fieldNames>Date_Signed__c</fieldNames>
        <fieldNames>Counter_Signed_Date__c</fieldNames>
        <fieldNames>NDA_Signed__c</fieldNames>
        <info>NDA fully executed. Record the date the counterparty signed and the date DPEG counter-signed, and attach the executed file. Signed is what releases information - once EVERY NDA on this disposition reads Signed, the disposition can move past the NDA stage.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Signed</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <pathAssistantSteps>
        <info>This party refused to sign, so nothing is shared with them. Declined is a dead end for this NDA record, not for the disposition: a declined NDA counts as neither outstanding nor signed, so it no longer holds the disposition at the NDA stage. Keep the record - it is the evidence that this party refused. Do not delete it.</info>
        <picklistValueName>Declined</picklistValueName>
    </pathAssistantSteps>
    <recordTypeName>Disposition_NDA</recordTypeName>
</PathAssistant>
